cff-version: 1.2.0
message: "If you use pyflightstream, please cite it using this metadata."
title: "pyflightstream: version-aware, didactic Python driver for the FlightStream panel-method solver"
type: software
authors:
  - family-names: Neves
    given-names: Geovana
license: MIT
# The version and the release date are set together, always. Between
# releases this file carries a development version and NO date-released,
# because a development tree has no release date; it once stated the
# v0.3.0 date beside version 0.4.0.dev0, so a citation generated from
# that tree asserted a release date for an artifact that was never
# released (REV010-015). This is a RELEASE tree: the version carries no
# .dev0 and a date-released stands beside it, which is the pair the
# guard in tests/test_metadata_currency.py enforces in both
# directions. The release commit sets the two together the other way
# and this sentence flips with them; it is written as a statement
# about the tree rather than as a rule so that it cannot quietly stop
# describing the file it heads. It did stop once, on 2026-08-11, when
# the version moved to .dev0 and this paragraph still said RELEASE:
# the guard reads the two fields and cannot read prose, which is the
# residual the wording was chosen to make visible rather than remove.
# The archive DOIs below are one per release from v0.3.0, newest first,
# with the concept DOI last. v0.1.0 and v0.2.0 are absent: v0.2.0 was the
# first release to archive, and its version DOI was not recorded here at
# the time. The concept DOI resolves to the newest archived version
# either way, so nothing points at a release that cannot be cited; the
# two missing rows are a gap in this list rather than in the archive. A version DOI is minted from the GitHub release, so
# it is always recorded one commit AFTER the tag it names. Older ones
# stay, because those releases did happen and are still what a citation
# of them should resolve to.
version: 0.10.1
date-released: 2026-09-02
repository-code: "https://github.com/nevesgeovana/pyflightstream"
